        {
          "id": "H1",
          "hypothesis": "z=1 beats point estimate on the primary endpoint in coupled and decoupled regimes",
          "outcome": "−3.8 pp and −1.8 pp",
          "verdict": "NOT SUPPORTED"
        },
        {
          "id": "H2",
          "hypothesis": "Replicate-based self-calibration restores bound coverage",
          "outcome": "Coverage 88.0% → 97.3%; primary −2.9 pp; feasible +4.5 pp",
          "verdict": "PARTLY SUPPORTED"
        },
        {
          "id": "H3",
          "hypothesis": "When uncertainty is homogeneous, CB-PFR and matched uniform margin select identically",
          "outcome": "20,000 / 20,000 identical selections",
          "verdict": "SUPPORTED"
        },
        {
          "id": "H4",
          "hypothesis": "Comparison with tuned margin and adaptive allocation",
          "outcome": "Tuned-margin primary gain ≤1.0 pp; adaptive allocation improves primary endpoint by about 8–19 pp in 7/8 regimes",
          "verdict": "MIXED; ALLOCATION IS STRONGER"
        }
      ],
      "headlineFindings": [
        "At z=1, truly feasible selections increase by about 3.5–25.3 percentage points versus point-estimate ranking across the eight synthetic regimes.",
        "The primary feasible-and-near-optimal endpoint is lower for CB-PFR z=1 in six of eight regimes.",
        "When both ranking rules are tuned for the primary endpoint, CB-PFR is within about 1 percentage point of a tuned uniform margin.",
        "At a matched 95% feasibility target, CB-PFR gains 2.1–15.6 percentage points over uniform margin when uncertainty varies independently of objective desirability; it ties under homogeneous uncertainty and loses about 0.5–1.3 points in the coupled regime.",
        "Self-calibration brings arbitrary-candidate bound coverage from 88.0% to 97.3% against a 97.5% nominal target, but selected-candidate coverage remains below nominal.",
        "All T7 regimes are author-generated synthetic data; T7 was self-registered in the working session, was not externally timestamped, and has not been independently replicated."
      ],
